<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="stages-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="stages-title">Find your stage</h2>
      <p>These stages are our own rule of thumb, not a legal test. A single registration or a notice can move you up regardless of size.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="stage-list">
      <li>
        <p class="stage-list__tag">Stage 1</p>
        <h3>Just you, few transactions, not registered for GST</h3>
        <p><strong>Usually enough:</strong> a separate bank account, a simple record, and yearly help with your income tax return.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for:</strong> whether you already need GST, for example when selling online or to other states.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="stage-list__tag">Stage 2</p>
        <h3>Regular sales, registered for GST, perhaps one helper</h3>
        <p><strong>Usually enough:</strong> monthly bookkeeping and GST return support, from a part-time or outside accountant.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for:</strong> purchases that do not appear in your GSTR-2B, and who keeps the data file.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="stage-list__tag">Stage 3</p>
        <h3>Staff, credit customers, stock, and TDS to deduct</h3>
        <p><strong>Usually enough:</strong> monthly books with reconciliations, payroll, TDS and a short monthly report.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for:</strong> cash tied up in overdue customers and slow stock.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p class="stage-list__tag">Stage 4</p>
        <h3>Several locations, a loan or investor, or a company structure</h3>
        <p><strong>Usually enough:</strong> a firm or in-house team, an audit where required, and regular management reports.</p>
        <p><strong>Watch for:</strong> reserved work, which needs a qualified professional.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="min-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="min-title">The minimum worth paying for</h2>
      <p>If your budget is tight, prioritise in this order:</p>
      <ol class="mini-steps test-steps">
        <li>Filing what you must, on time</li>
        <li>A monthly bank reconciliation</li>
        <li>A list of who owes you</li>
        <li>A yearly review of your accounts and tax position</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="para">Dashboards and detailed reports come after these.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Do it yourself, or pay?</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Often fine to do yourself</dt><dd>Recording sales and bills, keeping receipts, and {% routeLink "/services/msme-udyam-registration/", "Udyam registration" %}, which is free on the official portal.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Often worth paying for</dt><dd>Setting up your books correctly at the start, GST reconciliation, your first TDS or payroll set-up, and year-end accounts.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Get qualified help</dt><dd>Notices, audits, and anything that needs certification.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="mis-title">
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h2 id="mis-title" class="first-h2">Mistakes small businesses make with accountants</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Visiting once a year with a bag of bills.</strong> Nothing can be fixed by then, and the fee for reconstruction may be higher than monthly help.</li>
      <li><strong>Assuming "my CA handles it".</strong> Find out exactly what they handle. Many arrangements cover filing, not bookkeeping or advice.</li>
      <li><strong>Letting family or a single employee hold everything.</strong> Keep access and copies with the owner.</li>
      <li><strong>Treating the MSME label as a tax status.</strong> The Udyam limits (micro up to ₹2.5 crore investment and ₹10 crore turnover, as published on the portal) do not decide your GST or tax duties.</li>
    </ul>
